%I A398356 #13 Sep 03 2026 17:57:12
%S A398356 0,1,1,2,0,20,192,883,11840,179360,79803,7602488,91335778
%N A398356 Number of ways to arrange 1..2n in a circle, up to rotation and reflection, such that the product of any two adjacent entries plus 1 is prime.
%C A398356 For n >= 2, equivalently, a(n) is the number of Hamiltonian cycles in the graph with vertex set {1..2n} in which distinct i and j are adjacent if and only if i*j + 1 is prime. This is the graph underlying A397619.
%C A398356 There are no valid arrangements of 1..m for odd m: if i and j are distinct odd positive integers then i*j + 1 is even and at least 4, hence composite, so no two odd entries can be adjacent, and for odd m there are (m+1)/2 > m/2 odd entries among 1..m, too many to keep pairwise nonadjacent in a circle of m entries. This is why only even circle sizes are considered.
%C A398356 In any valid arrangement of 1..2n the n odd entries and the n even entries strictly alternate; in particular no two even entries are ever adjacent.
%C A398356 a(4) = 0: in the circle 1..8 the entry 8 can have only one odd neighbor, 5, since 8*1 + 1 = 9, 8*3 + 1 = 25 and 8*7 + 1 = 57 are composite while 8*5 + 1 = 41 is prime; as no two even entries can be adjacent, both neighbors of 8 would have to be odd, which is impossible.
%C A398356 The terms are not monotonic: a(9) = 179360 but a(10) = 79803. In the circle of 1..20 the entry 19 can be adjacent only to 10 and 12 (19*10 + 1 = 191 and 19*12 + 1 = 229 are prime, while 19*20 + 1 = 381 = 3*127 is not), so both neighbors of 19 are forced. In the circle of 1..22 the entry 19 gains a third possible neighbor, 22, since 19*22 + 1 = 419 is prime, and the count rises again.
%e A398356 For n = 2 the only arrangement of 1..4 up to rotation and reflection is (1, 2, 3, 4), since 1*2 + 1 = 3, 2*3 + 1 = 7, 3*4 + 1 = 13 and 4*1 + 1 = 5 are all prime, so a(2) = 1.
%o A398356 (PARI) a(n) = {if(n==0, return(0)); if(n==1, return(1)); my(m=2*n, c=0); forperm([2..m], p, my(ok=isprime(1*p[1]+1) && isprime(p[m-1]*1+1)); for(i=1, m-2, ok = ok && isprime(p[i]*p[i+1]+1)); if(ok, c++)); c/2} \\ Very slow, for n <= 6 or so; entry 1 is fixed to remove rotations, halving removes reflections
%Y A398356 Cf. A051252, A397619 (linear arrangements).
%K A398356 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398356 0,4
%A A398356 _Andre Ruiz Loera_, Jul 26 2026
